cards.body:
POJK 8/2023 lets a bank apply **simplified CDD** to customers and transactions it has assessed as low risk. Even then the bank must still:
- identify and verify the customer and any beneficial owner;
- know the purpose of the relationship;
- monitor transactions and update data in line with the risk.

Simplified CDD stops applying the moment laundering, terrorism financing or proliferation financing is suspected. The bank keeps a list of customers on simplified CDD and tells OJK about its own simplified procedure.

In press release **SP 190/OJK/GKPB/XII/2023**, dated **16 December 2023**, OJK said it had used its power under the 2023 financial sector law (the P2SK Law) to **order banks to block accounts** identified as used for illegal activity, including online gambling. In the previous **three months** it had ordered **more than 4,000** online gambling accounts blocked.

OJK also:
- asked banks to build systems that profile gambling behaviour so they can spot and block it early on their own;
- asked banks to strengthen **CDD and EDD** to identify customers linked to gambling or other crimes;
- stressed that where transactions do not fit the customer's profile, the bank must act quickly, including by **reporting an LTKM to PPATK**.

**The lesson:** a block ordered by an authority does not replace the bank's own duty to notice, analyse and report.

PPATK's public page on the DTTOT explains how Indonesia applies **targeted financial sanctions** under Law 9/2013, in line with **FATF Recommendation 6** and the UN Security Council resolutions on terrorism, including resolutions **1267 (1999)** and **1373 (2001)**.

The page sets out:
- listing by **Central Jakarta District Court** designation, and the procedures for extending or removing a listing;
- **immediate freezing** of all funds owned or controlled by listed persons, objections to a freeze, and an exception for **basic necessities**;
- that the list is also sent to **Customs** so cash carried by listed people can be stopped at the border;
- a **2015 joint regulation** of the Supreme Court, the foreign ministry, the police, the counter-terrorism agency (BNPT) and PPATK that governs the process.

PPATK also links the **proliferation financing list** from the same page. When Indonesia joined the FATF in 2023, PPATK noted that proliferation financing had been one of the three areas of its fast-track action plan.

Under POJK 8/2023 a bank must protect itself from **insiders** being used for laundering, terrorism financing or proliferation financing:

- **Pre-employment screening:** high standards when hiring permanent and non-permanent staff, including senior officers and experts, from the **lowest grade up to one level below** the Board of Directors and the Board of Commissioners.
- **Know your employee:** understand and monitor staff profiles from the lowest level **up to and including** the directors and commissioners.
- **Training at least yearly**, continuous and periodic, covering:
  - the laws and regulations on the programme;
  - techniques, methods and typologies of ML, TF and PF;
  - the bank's policies and procedures and **each employee's role**;
  - other material where needed.
- Training may be in-house, with partners such as industry associations or PPATK, or by sending staff to external courses.
- The bank must **evaluate** the training.

Examiners test evidence, not intentions. Keep ready:
- board and commissioner minutes where AML/CFT/CPF was discussed;
- the latest risk assessment and proof it reached OJK on time;
- the policy review, approvals and change log;
- training attendance by unit and by joining date, plus evaluation results;
- screening logs, freeze and nil reports, LTKM statistics;
- independent test reports and how findings were closed.
